WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.909
I'm Matt Schwartz,
I was an MBA in Stanford's 2017 class and

00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:07.670
I'm the co founder and CEO of Afresh.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.806
&gt;&gt; And I'm Nathan Fenner,
MBA Class of 2017, and

00:00:10.806 --> 00:00:13.523
I'm the co founder and
president of Afresh.

00:00:13.523 --> 00:00:16.990
[MUSIC]

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.216
&gt;&gt; Around the world,

00:00:18.216 --> 00:00:22.976
hundreds of billions of dollars of
fresh food are thrown away every year.

00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:27.831
And that fresh food represents millions
and millions of tons of greenhouse

00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:32.625
gases that are created both in
the landfills after they're thrown away.

00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:38.290
And also in the energy that's used to
grow, distribute, produce, that food.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:41.294
It's a really corny story that
Nathan hates when I share.

00:00:41.294 --> 00:00:44.546
When I first met him,
it was at an adamant weekend to the GSB.

00:00:44.546 --> 00:00:47.413
After spending a few days with Nathan,
I called my mom and

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:50.002
I told her I'm going to start
a company with this guy.

00:00:50.002 --> 00:00:52.298
&gt;&gt; But
I think I chose building this company for

00:00:52.298 --> 00:00:54.160
the opportunity to work with Matt,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.523
I think Matt was frequently dismissed at
business school because he's so honest.

00:00:58.523 --> 00:01:02.784
But I was just thoroughly impressed with
him, it's saw someone who I was really

00:01:02.784 --> 00:01:06.500
kind of my counter point in terms of
my values and how I saw the world.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.401
&gt;&gt; And the summer between our
years at the MBA program.

00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:13.763
Nathan and I signed up for an independent
research study with a GSB lecturer in

00:01:13.763 --> 00:01:18.050
which we did this big investigation
of the fresh food supply chain.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.622
Talking to a bunch of businesses,

00:01:19.622 --> 00:01:23.110
there was just an absolute
technology gap across the board.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.510
And that in turn culminated in us
identifying the immediate opportunity for

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:28.066
Afresh.

00:01:28.066 --> 00:01:29.800
&gt;&gt; A mentor once told me, look for

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.080
areas that are really
under-penetrated by technology.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.573
Find that field where that initial
technological innovation really,

00:01:36.573 --> 00:01:38.280
really moves the needle.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.103
And so what we do as a company is
we've built the first of its kind

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:48.670
Fresh Food First supply chain
technology for the Fresh Food Industry.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.932
&gt;&gt; Food more so than any other industry,
shapes the health of people and

00:01:52.932 --> 00:01:53.750
our planet.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.840
It'd be better around the world
if people ate more fresh food.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.670
At the same time,
fresh food is really costly.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:04.260
One of the biggest drivers of that cost
is the fact that so much of it is wasted.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.630
With what we're doing at Afresh, we
prevented on an order of 10 million pounds

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.210
of cumulative food waste
reduction at this point.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:16.642
I think the ability to go to work every
day knowing that every ounce of sweat that

00:02:16.642 --> 00:02:21.226
we put in, has that positive impact, is
the thing that motivates us in the team.

00:02:21.226 --> 00:02:25.475
[MUSIC]

